{% set homeowner_guide %}
<p>Quick reference for Priceville homeowners:</p>

<p><strong>New construction owners (homes under 15 years):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Builder gutters are undersized — expect problems within 5-10 years</li>
<li>Check downspout count: should have one per 30-40 feet of gutter</li>
<li>If gutters overflow during heavy rain, the problem is capacity, not blockage</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Established home owners:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Original sectional gutters from the 80s/90s are past replacement time</li>
<li>Check fascia behind gutters for soft spots — rot hides there</li>
<li>Gutter hangers loosen over time; sagging is a sign</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Near the river?</strong> Higher water tables mean soil saturates faster. Standard downspout extensions may not move water far enough. Consider underground drains routing water 30-50+ feet from foundation.</p>

<p><strong>Seasonal timing:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>March:</strong> Pre-storm season inspection</li>
<li><strong>November:</strong> Post-leaf drop cleaning</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Warning signs:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Overflow during moderate rain = undersized or blocked</li>
<li>Visible sagging = failing hangers</li>
<li>Staining on siding = chronic overflow</li>
<li>Musty smell in crawlspace = drainage not reaching far enough</li>
</ul>

